    `There is a season in the spiritual life that very few people talk about honestly because it sounds too much like a loss of faith to name it publicly. It is the season when God feels absent — when prayer feels like talking to a wall, when Scripture feels like words on a page rather than the living voice of Someone who is there, when the warmth and nearness that once characterised the relationship has given way to a grey, flat silence that no spiritual effort seems to penetrate. The worship does not lift you. The prayer does not connect. The silence is the loudest thing in the room. This experience has a long history and a proper name. The mystics called it the dark night of the soul. The psalmists called it being forgotten, being hidden from, being forsaken. And crucially — they called it these things in prayers addressed to the God they felt had gone silent. They did not walk away. They brought the silence itself to God. Psalm 22 — the psalm Jesus quoted from the cross — opens with one of the most honest prayers in all of Scripture: my God, my God, why have you forsaken me? I cry out by day but you do not answer. This is not a failure of faith. This is faith in its most stripped-down, most honest, most costly form — the continued addressing of a God who feels absent, by a person who refuses to stop addressing Him even when the feeling of His absence is the dominant reality. Isaiah 45:15 gives us the theological category for this experience: truly you are a God who hides Himself. God's hiddenness is not incidental to the biblical portrait of Him. It is part of it. He is a God who sometimes veils His presence — not because He has withdrawn, but because the hiddenness is doing something that the felt presence cannot do. The faith that operates in the felt presence of God is real faith. The faith that continues in the felt absence of God is a deeper faith — the faith that trusts the character of God when the experience of God is not available to confirm it. Habakkuk 2:3 gives us the most practical instruction available for the season of silence: wait for it. The revelation awaits an appointed time. It will certainly come and will not delay — which, given that it is described as lingering, means not that it will come quickly but that when it comes it will be exactly on time, not late in any meaningful sense. The appointed time is God's time, and God's time is not calibrated by the clock we are watching. The silence is not the end of the story. It is a chapter in it — and the chapter has an appointed end, a specific moment when the silence breaks and the revelation arrives, with the precision of a God who is never late to His own appointments.`
